{"type":"event","event":{"type":"tool_completed","toolCallId":"call_288c9f806c8b4da0b8b24456","toolName":"read_file","result":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 244 lines\n1: Completed-GMT: 2026-10-11 08:47:28 GMT\n2: Completed-Local: 2026-10-11 15:47:28 Asia/Bangkok\n3: \n4: # Adversarial review: slice 6c-pre, the stack kernel's tail-completion seam\n5: \n6: Reviewer: claude-fable-5-1 (non-author family). Author: codex gpt-6.1-sol.\n7: Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6cpre`, branch `srvm-phase6cpre`,\n8: HEAD 7f1cf08e, diff `git diff master...HEAD` (two files: `stack.cljc` modified,\n9: `stack_tail_completion_test.cljc` added). Read-only: no tracked file was\n10: changed; every mutant was applied to `stack.cljc` from a byte-verified backup\n11: (`collab/6cpre-reviewer-stack.backup.cljc`, `cmp` identical to HEAD's file) and\n12: restored after each run with `cmp` plus `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src/`. Final\n13: state: `cmp` identical, `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src/ test/ docs/` clean.\n14: \n15: Provenance of runs. This review was done in two sittings of the same reviewer\n16: identity on 2026-10-11 (the first hit a session limit after its mutant sweep\n17: had completed; its logs are `collab/6cpre-reviewer-mutant-01..18.log`,\n18: summary `collab/6cpre-reviewer-mutants.log`, baseline\n19: `collab/6cpre-reviewer-baseline.log`, 14:04-14:37 local). The second sitting\n20: re-verified the backup and the clean tree, ran eight further mutants\n21: (`collab/6cpre-reviewer-mutant2-01..08.log`, summary\n22: `collab/6cpre-reviewer-mutants2.log`), the handoff probe\n23: (`collab/6cpre-reviewer-handoff-repro.clj`, log\n24: `collab/6cpre-reviewer-handoff-repro.log`), kondo and cljstyle. Every mutant\n25: run was `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.debruijn.stack-tail-completion-test -n\n26: yin.vm.debruijn.stack-test -n yin.vm.debruijn.stack-effects-test -n\n27: yin.vm.debruijn.stack-parity-test -n yin.vm.semantic-ffi-test -n\n28: yin.vm.continuation-invoke-test` (baseline: 87 tests, 821 assertions, 0\n29: failures, 0 errors, matching the orchestrator's count). Not run by me: Dart,\n30: Node, the full regression lane.\n31: \n32: ## Findings\n33: \n34: ### F1 — MEDIUM | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc:898-923, 2088-2104, 2287-2290, 2963-2981` (not in the diff) | the version-2 handoff silently drops the new marker for the stack lane\n35: \n36: Evidence. `stack-registers-v2` (lift) builds the wire record from a fixed key\n37: set (`:yin.k/layout :image :pc :frames :stack :continuation [:store-of]`) and\n38: never reads `:resume-mode`; `stack-payload-v2` and the `:stack` arm of the\n39: lower (2963) never write it back; the `:stack` validator's `closed!` set\n40: (2287) would refuse `:yin.k/resume-mode` if it were ever carried. The register\n41: lane does carry it (978, 2137, 2314, 3007). Reproduced end to end with\n42: `collab/6cpre-reviewer-handoff-repro.clj` (a `(let [s …] (let [c …]\n43: (stream/next! c)))` whose inner application is `:tail? true`, stack kernel,\n44: lift by `v2-support/lift`, lower by `handoff/resume-task`):\n45: \n46: ```\n47: source blocked? true | site [:call 1 true] | resume-mode :return-result | K depth 2\n48: export status :ok\n49: resume status :ok\n50: resumed entry keys (:continuation :cursor-ref :format :frames :hash :image :pc :reason :segment :stack :stream-id)\n51: RESULT marker-before :return-result marker-after nil\n52: ```\n53: \n54: Consequence. After a v2 handoff the same tail park resumes by the old push\n55: path and runs the layout syntax after the call (the exact behaviour §4.3 calls\n56: non-canonical), so one blocked task now delivers differently before and after\n57: handoff. On master both sides agreed (both pushed at pc). The slice therefore\n58: introduces an observable divergence across the one carrier that exists today.\n59: Not a bug in `stack.cljc`; outside the brief's file scope (the brief said\n60: `stack.cljc` only), so the author could not fix it, but the report does not\n61: mention it (brief rule: record ambiguities you hit; §9 verification honesty).\n62: \n63: Correction (one of): (a) carry `:yin.k/resume-mode` on the v2 stack lane as the\n64: register lane does (lift at ~898, validator `closed!` optional set at ~2287\n65: plus a `contains? #{:return-result}` check mirroring 2329, lower at ~2963 and\n66: ~2088), with a `handoff-v2-*` round-trip test on a tail park; or (b) an\n67: Architect ruling that the v2 carrier is frozen and the window until the\n68: canonical carrier of §4.3 lands (6c) is accepted, recorded in the design or the\n69: report. My recommendation is (a): it is four small touch points and removes a\n70: state-dependent semantic split. Either way the decision should be explicit\n71: before merge.\n72: \n73: ### F2 — MEDIUM | `test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_tail_completion_test.cljc:29-37` | the empty-K obligations are not tested in a way that can fail\n74: \n75: Evidence. `empty-k-halts-with-result` is the only test with an empty `K`, and\n76: it delivers `nil`. The parked entry's own stack is `[]` and it has no\n77: `:store-of` (`registers` only adds one when the vm has it). So:\n78: \n79: - Mutant M08 (`halt :value`/`:stack` taken from `(peek (:stack vm))` instead of\n80:   the delivered value) SURVIVED all six suites; mutant N04 (seam-only: empty-K\n81:   halt delivers constant `nil`) SURVIVED. The design's \"halts with `[value]`\"\n82:   is asserted only for `value = nil`.\n83: - Mutant M09 (empty-K halt keeps `:store-of`) SURVIVED; N05 (seam-only: keep\n84:   the entry's `:store-of`) SURVIVED. The `(nil? (:store-of done))` assertion is\n85:   vacuous because the entry's `:store-of` is already nil.\n86: \n87: The report's sentence \"cannot accidentally treat nil as missing delivery\" is\n88: the wrong lesson: delivering only nil is what lets a wrong-value halt survive.\n89: The code itself is correct (`return-transition` halts with `[val]` and\n90: `:store-of nil`); the gap is in the test.\n91: \n92: Correction. Add one `stack-restore` case with `:continuation []`, a non-nil\n93: `:store-of` on the entry (as `frame-entry` already does) and a non-nil value,\n94: asserting `:stack [7]`, `(vm/value) 7`, `:halted? true`, `:store-of nil`; or\n95: extend `empty-k-halts-with-result` to append `7` and keep the nil case too.\n96: \n97: ### F3 — LOW | `test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_tail_completion_test.cljc:45-46, 50-55` | the inner frame's `:return-pc 4` coincides with the parked entry's `:pc 4`\n98: \n99: Evidence. `parked-call` parks at pc 4 (`[:call 1 tail?]` is index 3) and\n100: `frame-entry`'s innermost frame also has `:return-pc 4`, so the assertion\n101: `(= 4 (:pc done))` in `return-pops-only-inner-frame` holds whether or not the\n102: return transition sets the pc. Mutant N08 (seam-only: pc left at the entry pc\n103: after the transition) was caught only by `tail-in-operand-position-skips-layout`,\n104: and only as an error (the machine ran on from pc 10 with a popped `K`), not by\n105: the test that names the obligation.\n106: \n107: Correction. Use a `:return-pc` different from 4 (e.g. 2) on the inner frame.\n108: \n109: ### F4 — INFO | `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:581` | pre-existing `:halted? false` on restore is untested by the six suites\n110: \n111: Mutant M18 (drop `:halted? false` from the restored registers) SURVIVED. This\n112: line existed before the slice (the diff only moves it) and is outside the\n113: seam; its own comment names the scenario (a driver resuming a halted machine\n114: through `engine/resume-continuation`). Not a 6c-pre defect; noted so the\n115: survivor count is honest. No change requested in this slice.\n116: \n117: ### F5 — INFO | report | mutant claim is true for the eight the author ran, not in general\n118: \n119: The author's \"No mutant survives\" holds for the author's eight mutants (I did\n120: not re-run them; their logs are `collab/6cpre-mutation-1..8.log`). Of my 26,\n121: four survived (M08, M09, M18, N04, N05 counting seam-only variants: three\n122: distinct weaknesses, F2 and F4). The report's acceptance list is the author's\n123: own numbering (the design's 6c-pre paragraph is prose); that reading is\n124: correct and stated plainly. Everything else in the report that I checked is\n125: accurate: lane counts for the six JVM suites, the Dart NOT RUN with the exact\n126: error, the fixture correction on the first run, the redundant-let cleanup, the\n127: \"constructed native-entry\" caveat on the FFI tests.\n128: \n129: ## Answers 1-5\n130: \n131: **1. The seam.** The marker is minted in one place, `park-entry-fns`\n132: (`stack.cljc:594-597`), from the instruction at the *current* pc (`run-effect`\n133: passes the pre-advance vm, so `(:pc vm)` is the call site) and only when that\n134: instruction is `[:call _ true]`. All three builders (`:stream/put`,\n135: `:stream/next`, `:module/require`) and the engine's `:stream/poll` fallback to\n136: the `:stream/next` builder inherit it. `stack-restore` (`:582-584`) routes a\n137: marked entry through `return-transition`, which is the former `:return` arm\n138: verbatim (`:pc (:return-pc frame)`, `:frames (:frames frame)`, `:stack (conj\n139: (subvec stack 0 (:stack-base frame)) val)`, `pop` K, `:store-of (:store-of\n140: frame)`; empty K: `:halted? true :stack [val] :value val :store-of nil`). The\n141: `:return` arm now calls the same function with `(peek stack)`: no divergence\n142: between the seam and `:return`. The old park/restore path (`:584`) is\n143: byte-equivalent to the removed code for unmarked entries (the `restored` map\n144: plus the conj). Paths where a marked entry still executes the layout syntax:\n145: only after a v2 handoff (F1). Non-tail parks: no marker (`non-tail-park-keeps-\n146: successor`, mutant M02 killed also by `stack-effects-test`). Captured\n147: continuations: `:current-continuation` and `:resume` build payloads with\n148: `registers` directly (`:862, :868`), never marked, and a `:call` of a\n149: continuation value goes through `stack-restore` with that unmarked payload, so\n150: it still pushes at pc. Synchronous results: the non-blocked branch of\n151: `run-effect` and the `:host-fn` plain-result branch are untouched; a tail host\n152: call that returns synchronously still pushes and runs the following layout\n153: syntax (pre-existing, not changed by the design's seam, which speaks of parks\n154: only). The `:halt` opcode is unchanged (it keeps the whole stack; only\n155: `:return`'s empty-K halt normalises to `[val]`, as before).\n156: \n157: **2. Retained-FFI and response decoding order.** Preserved: the `:request-sent`\n158: branch runs first and re-parks the *same* entry via `ffi/response-wait-entry`\n159: (marker kept, mutant M12 killed), then `:call-id` removes the parked record and\n160: decodes via `ffi/call-result`, and only then the marked/unmarked delivery\n161: happens (M11, M13, M17 killed). Host-fn effects are the only real producers of\n162: the marker. FFI parks (`:ffi-call`, `:644`) use `registers` directly and are\n163: never marked; the instruction has no tail flag. This matches the register\n164: kernel, whose `tail?` is also `(and (= :call op) (true? (nth instruction 4)))`\n165: (`debruijn_register_effects.cljc:100`), so there is no asymmetry. The two FFI\n166: seam tests are therefore synthetic entries (a tail `:call` entry with\n167: `:request-sent`/`:call-id` added by hand); the report says so. The engine's\n168: writer retry (`apply-put`, `engine.cljc:1919-1933`) re-wakes the entry object\n169: itself, so the marker survives an in-process retry.\n170: \n171: **3. Mutants.** See the table. 26 applied by me; 21 killed, 5 survived (two of\n172: which, N04/N05, are seam-only restatements of M08/M09 to localise the gap).\n173: \n174: **4. Scope.** `git diff master...HEAD --name-status`: `M stack.cljc`, `A\n175: stack_tail_completion_test.cljc`; nothing else. No existing test touched, no\n176: semantic-register or register-kernel edit, no design edit. kondo on both files:\n177: 0 errors, 0 warnings (one pre-existing info). cljstyle check: exit 0.\n178: Portability by reading: the source change uses `nth` on a three-element\n179: instruction vector guarded by `(= :call (first site))`, `true?`, `cond->`,\n180: `subvec`, `peek`/`pop` on vectors; no reader conditionals, no private\n181: cross-namespace access, no `for`, no float negation, no multi-key `assoc` on\n182: nil. The test uses `tu/make-stream`, `tu/secret`, `apply2/success-response`,\n183: `stream/append!`, vector literals; `(subvec (:stack entry) 0 1)` on a vector.\n184: Nothing host-specific found; Dart remains unrun (author's lane NOT RUN,\n185: orchestrator to rerun).\n186: \n187: **5. Design readings and honesty.** The author read the 6c-pre paragraph as\n188: prose and self-numbered the obligations; correct, and declared. The author\n189: read \"halt with the value when K is empty\" as \"assert with nil\"; that is the\n190: weak reading (F2). The author did not consider the existing v2 carrier for the\n191: marked entry (F1); the design's carried shape in §4.3 is for the canonical\n192: lowering, so this is a gap in both the brief and the design's slicing rather\n193: than a misreading. Report claims I checked are honest (F5).\n194: \n195: ## Mutant table\n196: \n197: Status is against the six JVM suites named above unless noted. \"Named test\"\n198: lists the failing tests; TIMEOUT means the run hung past 900 s (an infinite\n199: loop in the shared `:return` arm) and was killed by the driver.\n200: \n201: | # | Mutation of `stack.cljc` | Status | Named test(s) | Log |\n202: |---|---|---|---|---|\n203: | M01 | marker condition → `false` (marker omitted) | KILLED | tail-park-is-marked, empty-k-halts-with-result, return-*, tail-in-operand-position-skips-layout, retained-ffi-reparks-before-return, ffi-response-decodes-before-return | reviewer-mutant-01 |\n204: | M02 | marker on every `:call` (non-tail too) | KILLED | non-tail-park-keeps-successor; stack-effects-test/a-blocking-primitive-effect-parks-after-the-call-site-test | 02 |\n205: | M03 | marker on every effect park | KILLED | non-tail-park-keeps-successor; stack-effects-test (2) | 03 |\n206: | M04 | `return-transition` does not pop K | KILLED (TIMEOUT: `:return` loops) | none named before hang | 04 |\n207: | N01 | seam-only: frame not popped | KILLED | return-pops-only-inner-frame | mutant2-01 |\n208: | M05 | base not truncated (shared) | KILLED (TIMEOUT) | escape-discards-pending-frames-test, tail-position-invocation-test before hang | 05 |\n209: | N02 | seam-only: base not truncated | KILLED | return-truncates-at-caller-base, tail-in-operand-position-skips-layout, ffi-response-decodes-before-return | mutant2-02 |\n210: | M06 | truncated to the outermost frame's base | KILLED | return-truncates-at-caller-base, ffi-response-decodes-before-return, stack-test/re-entry-after-return-test | 06 |\n211: | M07 | value not pushed (shared) | KILLED | 21 tests incl. return-truncates-at-caller-base, tail-in-operand-position-skips-layout | 07 |\n212: | N03 | seam-only: value not pushed | KILLED | empty-k-halts-with-result, return-truncates-at-caller-base, tail-in-operand-position-skips-layout, ffi-response-decodes-before-return | mutant2-03 |\n213: | M08 | empty-K halt value from `(peek stack)` instead of `val` | **SURVIVED** | — | 08 |\n214: | N04 | seam-only: empty-K halt delivers constant nil | **SURVIVED** | — | mutant2-04 |\n215: | M09 | empty-K halt keeps `:store-of` | **SURVIVED** | — | 09 |\n216: | N05 | seam-only: empty-K halt keeps the entry's `:store-of` | **SURVIVED** | — | mutant2-05 |\n217: | M10 | delivered twice (value conj'd again after the transition) | KILLED | empty-k-halts-with-result, return-truncates-at-caller-base, tail-in-operand-position-skips-layout, ffi-response-decodes-before-return | 10 |\n218: | M11 | retained re-park skipped (`:request-sent` branch disabled) | KILLED | retained-ffi-reparks-before-return; semantic-ffi-test/full-reque